<BaseLayout title="Privacy & Analytics" description="How Evidence to Decision handles website analytics and visitor preferences.">
<div class="archive-intro"><h1>Privacy &amp; Analytics</h1><p>Last updated October 24, 2026</p></div>
<div class="about-content prose">
<p><strong>Evidence to Decision</strong> is an independent blog by Haitao Pan, PhD. The website uses Google Analytics 4 to measure readership, including page views, approximate country or regional distribution, and general referral sources. These summaries help understand which scientific essays readers find useful.</p>
<h2>Optional website analytics</h2>
<p>Google Analytics is optional. We do not load the Google Analytics tag or send analytics data to Google unless you select <strong>Allow analytics</strong> in the website preference banner. If you choose <strong>No thanks</strong>, Google Analytics stays disabled on that browser. Your choice is stored in your browser and can be changed at any time using the <strong>Cookie settings</strong> link in the footer.</p>
<p>Google Analytics may use cookies and process technical information about your browser, pages visited, and approximate geographic location. Geographic reports are approximate and do not show the blog owner a visitor's street address or personal identity. Aggregate visit counts may not include readers who decline analytics or block tracking.</p>
<p>The Google Analytics tag is configured for analytics rather than personalized advertising. For more about Google's practices, see <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Google's Privacy Policy</a>.</p>
<h2>Comments and other services</h2>
<p>If interactive third-party commenting or email subscription services are enabled in the future, those services may have their own privacy policies and may process information that visitors voluntarily submit. Please do not post confidential clinical, study, or patient information in public comments.</p>
<h2>Your choices</h2>
<p><button type="button" class="privacy-preference-button" data-open-analytics-settings>Change analytics preferences</button></p>
<p>If you would like to read without analytics, choose <strong>No thanks</strong>. This does not affect access to any article or the RSS feed.</p>
</div>
</BaseLayout>
